  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday night through next Friday)\nIssued at 107 PM CDT Fri Sep 25 2026\n\nThe previously mentioned disturbance will continue moving\nnortheast and be centered just north of our area. This will assist\nin generating some isolated rain showers and thunderstorms on\nSaturday afternoon and evening. The high pressure will be weakened\nfurther and allow for slightly higher rain chances (30%) on\nSaturday afternoon and evening. \n\nA brief lull in the activity will be had through the remainder\nof the weekend and into Monday. However, long range models\ncontinue to show a strong low pressure developing across the\nsouthwestern U.S. and moving across New Mexico. Eventually, models\nbring this system across northwest Texas. A cold front will trail\nbehind this low and move into our area by mid week. Meanwhile, \nthe remnants of Hurricane Polo are depicted as moving in ahead of,\nor at the same time as, this low pressure system. Given this \nscenario, we may see an increase in thunderstorm activity across \nthe region in the Tuesday to Thursday timeframe as a result of \nboth systems. The heavy rain potential would be higher under this\nscenario as well. Rain chances are medium (30-50%) for this time \nperiod for the time being, but there remains considerable \nuncertainty in the forecast at this point. Cooler temperatures \ncloser to the middle 80s (high temperatures) are forecast for the \nmiddle and end of next week.",
